The Hubble tension is a disagreement over the universe's current expansion rate, the Hubble constant H0. The two leading ways of measuring it give different answers. The early-universe route fits the standard ΛCDM model to the cosmic microwave background and projects forward to today. Planck finds H0 = 67.4 ± 0.5 km/s/Mpc this way (arXiv:1807.06209), and baryon acoustic oscillation (BAO) plus Big Bang nucleosynthesis analyses give similar values. The late-universe route uses the distance ladder. Geometric anchors calibrate Cepheid variable stars, Cepheids calibrate Type Ia supernovae, and the supernovae reach the smooth Hubble flow. The SH0ES team finds H0 = 73.04 ± 1.04 km/s/Mpc this way (arXiv:2112.04510). The gap is about 5σ. If the quoted uncertainties are right, that is very unlikely to be a statistical fluke.

There are two broad explanations, and neither has won. One is unrecognized systematic errors. These could include Cepheid crowding and photometry, how Cepheid brightness depends on metallicity, or how the supernovae are standardized. Calibrating with the tip of the red giant branch instead of Cepheids gives an in-between H0 ≈ 69.8 km/s/Mpc (arXiv:1907.05922), which keeps that debate open. The other is physics beyond ΛCDM, such as early dark energy, extra relativistic species, modified recombination, or dark-sector interactions. Most such models ease the H0 gap only by straining fits to other data, such as large-scale structure (S8) or BAO (review: arXiv:2103.01183). Independent probes may settle it: gravitational-wave standard sirens, time delays in strongly lensed quasars, and JWST distance-ladder observations.

